tion is finished, and it's confirmed that the two frameworks solve fundamentally different problems. The canonical POT method adopts two different regularizations for the original cost matrix and its scaled version respectively, while GeomLoss uses the same cost matrix with the regularization parameter. For correct v3 calculation, two separate solves on the original cost matrix are required, with potentials extracted from the reg=0.1 solve on the original C.\n\nAfter checking the v3 code, I found that the unscaled potentials obtained from it correspond to an effective regularization of 0.1*C_max, which is inconsistent with the 0.1 regularization of GeomLoss's output.\n\nThis mismatched effective regularization is the core cause of v3's calculation error. The effective regularization strength of POT's configuration is far higher than that of GeomLoss's, leading to overly smooth coupling results.\n\nI've finalized the v4 optimization approach. To address the error caused by mismatched regularization settings, I'll use a simplified Sinkhorn solve configuration for each step, and given the loose quality gates (NRMSE ≤ 0.01), this approximation is valid and won't affect the final result quality.\n\nI've clarified that the batch transport Q is a weighting operation across reference batches for each candidate batch. After profiling the runtime distribution of the v4 workflow, I confirmed that the dominant cost comes from per-batch-pair Sinkhorn solves, and cutting the number of such solves in half can bring a 40%-50% speedup.\n\nTo implement the 40%-50% speedup we identified, I will use GeomLoss' PyTorch tensorized backend for Sinkhorn solves, and derive the coupling matrix from the obtained dual potentials to cut per-batch-pair Sinkhorn counts in half. I'm now verifying the exact formula for coupling calculation from dual potentials.\n\nI've completed the verification of the coupling calculation formula derived from dual potentials, confirming it can correctly compute the